Singelton Design Pattern:
Main Agenda of singelton design pattern is to create only one object per class.
so we should restrict someone from outside to create an object of a class directly
by using new Keyword. so we will make our class constructor private.so no one will
be able to create object of our class from outside of class.
we can implement singelton design pattern in many ways they are
1)Eager INITIALIZATION
->In Eager INITIALIZATION we will declare a private static final variable where we
will be directly create an object as this is final no one can change it and as it is
private no one outside the class can access it.
->Now create a public method which will return our same object everytime.
->The problem with Eager INITIALIZATION is we are creating object of our class upfront.
which will consume memory in HEAP Area even if no one uses it
2)Lazy Initialization
->Here we will be not initialization our variable directly
->In getInstance() method we will be checking if our variable is null then we will initialize
it.if not null directly return it.so we will be creating object only when needed
->This fails in Multithreaded environment
3)Using Synchronized method
->In LazyInitialization make getInstance() method synchronized so only one thread can access
the critical section at one time.
->Issue here is lets say we are calling getInstance() at thousand places then when one thread
is inside getInstance() method then remaining 999 threads should wait,This is performance issue
4)Using Double checked locking
->Using Double checked locking we remove synchronized from method level and add synchronized
at block level so remaining threads can access code inside getInstance() which donot require
locking easily.
->Issue here is still if at beginning 1000 threads reach synchronized block then they should go
through that block still performance issue
5)Using Bill pugh solution
->Create an inner static class which has static final variable and inside static getInstance()
return that final variable ,here no locking no performance issue.
